I have the very distinct memory that whenever I quaff from a
polymorphing fountain my character always turns into a Xorn. Without a
ring of polymorph control unless you stay in a room you can become
stuck. I was next to a store s wall escaping a mimic when polymorph
ended. The first two scrolls I read did little good, but I am sure it
was the third scroll I picked up as a Xorn that deposited me in the
next level. It is too good to be true, as if the RNG was bystepped and
the saving situation was hard coded. I lost an unidentified opera
cloack, but saved my invisibility cloak which is very good luck.

Now I was bitten by a wererat in the mines and am changing form
without control. It is annoying that calling #monster did not produce
a horde of aggressive brethren but instead very calmed rats! They
should have killed everything around me. As a wererat I was engulfed
by a vortex, missed the chance to drop my encumbrance inside it to see
what happens, it let me go in a few turns, rats did nothing and the
same also with elf zombies and a rock mole. But I have the very
annoying feeling that I had already played exactly the same scene,
only with a different character and I did lose stuff inside the vortex
then, while rats were fewer so I missed their behaviour.

The same has happened in the fourth level, I reach one room with four
or five monsters, locked door, one is a dead something, another is a
splashing puddle, my pet gets killed out of reach and the door is
blocked so that you cannot illuminate the full room. This time I was
killed in the door so I could not see what else was inside besides
some fountain or sink. It does not seem to be a special room, so it
may mean that the RNG is really producing the same series! I would
expect it to produce a very different series as time has passed. I as
a player may even be correlated to it! Heteroscedasticity... which
makes me think of Heiselberg and players reading their games s code...
I wonder is anyone has played the same game with a different brand of
RNG to see if it plays better.

